Always remember to set up your testing environment to allow assertions and to use the correct testing framework in your case, for your specific case. This translation is just a general guide. Some modifications will be needed in production environment to adapt to the context.--------------------You can output assertions based on comments starting with "@@@" by using regular expressions to match these lines and then building the proper assertion messages based on the condition in these comments.

The following Python code uses the `re` (Regular Expression) module to find all lines starting with "@@@" and then builds the Debug.Assert statements.

```python
import re

def find_asserstion_statements(file):
    with open(file, 'r') as f:
        text = f.readlines()
        for line in text:
            if line.strip().startswith('@@@'):
                comment = line.strip().replace('@@@', '').strip()
                expression = re.search(r"(\w+) (.+) (.+)", comment)
                if expression:
                    operator = expression.group(1)
                    left = expression.group(2)
                    right = expression.group(3)
                    assert_statement = f"Debug.Assert({left} {operator} {right});"
                    print(assert_statement)

find_asserstion_statements('file.cs')
```

Just replace 'file.cs' with your CSharp file.

This script will match conditions like "new last index of x is greater than old last index of x" in the comments. It will then build the string for the debug.assert statement.

It's note that Debug.Assert() should be used only for debugging. It can slow down the application. For production code use normal exception handling.--------------------Here is csharp code that uses the "Debug.Assert(...);"-statement:

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, "new last index of x is greater than or equal to old last index of x");
